{
  "_comment": "How a Catalan word on the page is turned into something the dictionary can be asked about. Read by lib/lookup.py; optional, like every file of this name. Wiktionary's Catalan carries the conjugation and the plurals, so an inflected word needs no rule. What it does not carry is a word with something written onto it: an elided article, preposition or pronoun in front (l'home, d'aigua, s'alça, m'agrada, n'hi) and the weak pronouns written after an infinitive, a gerund or an imperative, with a hyphen (menjar-lo, fer-ho, dona-m'ho, donar-me-la) or, after a vowel, an apostrophe (porta'l, veure'ls). All of these found nothing before this file.",
  "fold": "",
  "_affixes": "THE ELISIONS, both apostrophes (' and ’). THE WEAK PRONOUNS AFTER THE VERB, alone or in the clusters docs/lang/ca.md lists: the hyphen or the apostrophe that writes them on is unambiguous in Catalan, so no `after` is needed -- nothing else ends in -lo with a hyphen. A word takes at most two steps (lib/lookup.py MAX_PEEL): an elision and a cluster, or two hyphenated pronouns (donar-me-la). The contractions (al, del, pel, can) are pages of their own and need no rule.",
